  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Violetto correspondence.",
    "overview": "Blue framing, a cool blue desk and soft white reading paper make Japanese study feel like correspondence shared with Violetto. Upright classical headings and airy Japanese Mincho retain the letter-world character. The latest user preference makes blue predominant, adds small ribbon-red details and permits muted violet correspondence accents. It supersedes the earlier emerald/ivory direction and blanket purple prohibition; the original kickoff remains character inspiration, not absolute palette authority.\n\nVioletto is a visible study companion: prominent on arrival and Today, then quieter around active reading and retrieval. Her confirmed atlas, blonde braids, blue eyes, navy coat, ivory skirt, emerald brooch and red ribbons remain intact. Shiori / 栞 remains provisional. The visual revision preserves course content, persona, audio behavior and persistence identity (`shiori-japanese-v1`, `shiori-japanese`, `shiori-reply-`). The implementation is code-led; the direction is user-requested, but no pixel composition or new artwork was approved.\n",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Blue header and actions, cool paper, restrained correspondence accents and gold ornaments.",
      "An unfilled Today portrait arch and readable shared reaction bubble using existing Violetto art and voice.",
      "Open manuscript lesson on Today; focused study, numbered selections and fixed verdicts during retrieval.",
      "Upright serif headings, practical UI controls and honest learning evidence."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Color Has a Job Rule",
        "body": "Blue identifies the app and its actions; muted violet marks correspondence; emerald marks successful learning; ribbon marks small postal details, errors or seals. Support reveals do not become success merely because they have a colored background.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Language First Rule",
        "body": "Preserve readable Japanese, ruby spacing and explanation hierarchy before allocating space to companion art, stamps or metadata.",
        "section": "typography"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do use semantic theme tokens and inspect both Morning letter and Night post.",
      "Do make the predominantly blue letter world and Violetto visible at arrival while keeping Japanese primary during practice.",
      "Do retain the established font families, upright headings, airy Mincho and small typewriter accents.",
      "Do preserve keyboard focus, nearby gloss feedback and usable phone study controls.",
      "Do update this file and its sidecar together from the final CSS cascade and rendered evidence.",
      "Do treat Violetto as confirmed and Shiori as provisional, preserving course, persona and persistence truth.",
      "Do follow SHARED-SPEC batches A–C while keeping D/E palette and voice-route work deferred for approval."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't restore emerald primary actions or treat the earlier ban on purple as current guidance; blue leads, with restrained red and muted violet accents.",
      "Don't replace distinct learning surfaces with uniform card grids, decorative scores or marketing sections.",
      "Don't let companion art, postal ornament or continuous animation obstruct reading and retrieval.",
      "Don't present guided answers, correspondence levels, completion counts or roadmap labels as proven fluency.",
      "Don't describe this code-led design as an approved image composition or the working app name as settled.",
      "Don’t restore filled Today mentor/lesson cards, the lilac strip or filled desktop current-page pills; keep reaction bubbles clear of answers."
    ]
  }
}
